  "context_pill": "Partial Integration of Agricultural Income",
  "example_summary": "Tax liability computation under partial integration scheme for Mr. X across two age brackets: 40 years and 75 years",
  "source_provision": "### Statutory Summary Matrix: Partial Integration Scheme\n\n| Head / Provision | Statutory Basis | Applicability Threshold / Test | Legal Tax Consequence |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Agricultural Exemption** | Section 10(1) | Income derived from land situated in India & used for agricultural purposes | Completely exempt from Total Income |\n| **Net Ag Income Computation** | Section 10(1) | Gross Agricultural Receipts *less* Expenses exclusively incurred | Only Net Agricultural Income is considered |\n| **Partial Integration Condition 1** | Finance Act Rules | Net Agricultural Income > ₹ 5,000 p.a. | Trigger condition for partial integration |\n| **Partial Integration Condition 2** | Finance Act Rules | Non-Agricultural Total Income > Basic Exemption Limit | Trigger condition for partial integration |\n\n\n### Step-by-Step Partial Integration Tax Formula\n\n* **Step 1:** Tax on (Non-Agricultural Total Income + Net Agricultural Income) at regular slab rates.\n* **Step 2:** Tax on (Basic Exemption Limit + Net Agricultural Income) at regular slab rates.\n* **Step 3:** Net Tax Before Cess = **Step 1 Tax** - **Step 2 Tax**.\n* **Step 4 & 5:** Add Health and Education Cess @ 4% on Net Tax Liability.\n\n\n> [!IMPORTANT] Basic Exemption Limits under Regular (Opt-Out) Tax Regime\n> * **Resident Individual (< 60 years):** Basic Exemption Limit is ₹ 2,50,000.\n> * **Resident Senior Citizen (60 years to < 80 years):** Basic Exemption Limit is ₹ 3,00,000.\n> * **Resident Super Senior Citizen (80 years or more):** Basic Exemption Limit is ₹ 5,00,000.",
